WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.120
Each language has its own sound and structure,
and feels a certain way.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.400
Italian is musical, while German is harsher.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.800
And the same person fluent in both can seem
like they have two distinct personalities.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:22.960
But can language really influence who we are?

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.320
Linguistic relativity theory suggests that
the languages we pick up and use influence

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.960
how we see the world - the perception of our
surroundings, our sense of time, our memories,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.720
our behavior, and even our sense of self.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.800
First, there’s our perception.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.960
Each language has different words to describe
the things around us and these differences

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.480
change the way we perceive these things.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:54.440
For example, an Italian might see objects
as male or female, while an English speaker

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.920
won’t —a phenomenon that extends to the
perception of space.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.600
The Kuuk Thaayorre, an aboriginal tribe, does
not use left or right in their language.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.520
Instead, they rely on cardinal directions:
North, East, South, and West.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.560
They might say “Pass me the salt on the
South-west of the pepper”.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:22.160
It's probably no coincidence that they have
supernatural navigational skills.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.160
There is the sense of time.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.880
Because languages are written in different
directions, people who write left to right

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.000
will perceive the sequence of events accordingly.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.680
Speakers of Arabic and Hebrew might do so
the other way around.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.440
In the Amele language in Papua New Guinea,
there are 3 forms of past tense: Today’s

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.000
past tense, yesterday’s, and remote past
tense.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.880
To use the correct verb, speakers need to
pay close attention to when something has

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:57.960
happened — and hence may perceive the past
in greater detail.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.640
Language also affects our Memories.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.520
In English, if a child breaks a vase accidentally,
we may say “he broke the vase” — putting

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.760
the responsibility on the kid.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:14.240
In Spanish people would say “El jarrón
se rompió” or “The vase broke” — giving

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.720
more credit to the environment — a small
difference with big consequences.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.200
Studies show that speakers of languages like
Spanish that tend to consider these kinds

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.920
of events accidental are less likely to recall
the people involved.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.960
In languages that emphasize an intention,
the actors are remembered more clearly.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.960
And if things end up in court the accused
is more likely to be found guilty, if the

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:45.600
language used during the trial is active,
like English.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.640
Then there is our behavior.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:53.120
Languages are often associated with culture,
and with the context in which it was learned.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:59.760
As a result, those who speak various languages,
access different parts of themselves.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.760
One study on Spanish-English bilinguals showed
that people exhibit different personality

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.120
traits when switching their language.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:13.200
When speaking English, subjects were more
agreeable, but when they switched to Spanish

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.240
they were more neurotic.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.880
And finally, our sense of self.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.160
The words we speak also influence how we feel
about ourselves.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:29.040
Self-affirming language for example activates
brain areas linked to reward and self-perception

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:35.720
and can increase our confidence — an effect
that extends to how we perceive pain.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.760
Bilinguals report greater levels of pain when
they speak the language to which they relate

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:41.720
the most.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:47.160
The theory: if one language represents a bigger
part of their identity, they also perceive

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:53.000
the sensations inside their body with greater
intensity.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.840
With about 7000 languages all around us, nobody
knows exactly how they affect our minds.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:06.160
But they certainly frame our thoughts and
influence the way we look at the world.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:12.320
In Thai for example, the word for heart, 'jai',
is woven into their language to describe a

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.360
variety of human traits.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.760
Someone friendly is “jai dee”, literally
a good heart.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:24.440
Someone that’s patient is “jai yen”
a "cool heart " and someone generous, is “jai

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.360
gwang” a "big heart”.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:33.640
As Benjamin Lee Whorf, the linguist behind
the theory wrote: "Language is not merely

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:39.680
a reproducing instrument for voicing ideas
but rather is itself the shaper of ideas,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.240
the program and guide for the individual's
mental activity."

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:50.640
So, if you ever learn a new language, remember:
you're not just learning new words.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:57.160
You're opening your mind to a new way of seeing
and interacting with the world.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.240
What do you think?

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.440
Do you speak more than one language?

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:05.320
And if so, do you feel like your behavior
changes when you switch between them?

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.520
Tell us your thoughts and experiences

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:16.680
in
the comments below!

